# iOS → Android Port Playbook (DMVVM-C)

How to rebuild a SwiftUI iOS app (SwiftUI + SwiftData + Firebase + StoreKit 2)
as a native Android app (Kotlin + Jetpack Compose + Room + Firebase + Play Billing)
using the **exact same DMVVM-C (Dual-Write Models MVVM-C) architecture**, kept as its own repository.

**No Repository or UseCase layer on Android:** Android uses the same DMVVM-C pattern as iOS. ViewModels receive Room `@Dao`s (the Android equivalent of SwiftData's `ModelContext`) and `AppPreferences` in their constructor and call dual-write methods (`toEntity()`, `saveToFirestore()`, `saveToFirestoreThrowing()`, and rollback-safe copy-on-mutate helpers) **directly on the `@Serializable data class *Codable` domain models**.

The guiding principle: **audit and align upfront (Phase 0), then port the logic bottom-up and prove each layer with tests before building UI.** Pure business logic moves first (highest confidence, zero UI risk), then Dual-Write Models + Room persistence, then services, then the coordinator, then screens, then polish. By the time we touch UI, everything underneath is already green.

## Hard rule — review after every phase (do not skip)

A phase is **not done** when the Android code compiles, tests are green, or the
feature "looks close." It is done only after you **re-read the iOS source you
just ported**, compare it to the Android diff, and either match iOS or record an
intentional platform deviation.

**Do not start the next phase until this close-out is finished.**

After you finish implementing a phase, you MUST:

1. **Re-open the iOS files** this phase mapped. Do not review from memory. For
   every Android type/function you added or changed, open the corresponding Swift
   file and read it again.
2. **Source-diff line by line.** Copy strings (casing, punctuation, ellipses),
   Firestore/JSON key names, enum raw values and case coverage, default values,
   missing-field fallbacks, error messages, sort orders, numeric constants
   (durations, sizes, hex colors), and edge-case branches. List every delta —
   never eyeball "close enough."
3. **Fix or record.** For each delta: restore iOS behavior, **or** write it down
   as an intentional Android deviation with the reason (platform API, Play vs
   StoreKit, JVM vs Foundation, etc.). Unrecorded drift is a bug.
4. **Prove with tests that mirror iOS.** The phase test suite should cover the
   same inputs→outputs as the iOS tests for that layer. Green tests that do not
   assert iOS behavior are not a parity gate.
5. **Run it when the layer is visible.** Logic/data/services: JVM tests are the
   observe step. Coordinator/screens/polish: launch Android next to iOS (or
   screenshots) and exercise loading / empty / error / signed-in vs anonymous.
6. **Write a short parity log** in the Android repo (README phase table or a
   phase note): matched, fixed during review, deferred to a later phase,
   intentional deviations. Then — and only then — mark the phase complete.

If review finds a mismatch, **fix it in this phase.** Do not carry silent iOS
drift into later phases.

This rule applies to **every** iOS → Android port, not only the first time the
playbook was used.

**After every phase, also run the three-pass parity gate below.**

## Architecture mapping (iOS → Android 1:1)

| iOS (Swift) | Android (Kotlin) |
|-------------|------------------|
| `@main App` + `AppDependencies` | `MainActivity` / `Application` + `AppDependencies` |
| SwiftUI Views (`Views/<Feature>/`, `Views/Components/`) | Jetpack Compose + Material 3 (`ui/<feature>/`, `ui/components/`, `ui/theme/`) |
| `@MainActor @Observable final class *ViewModel` (`@State` / `@Bindable`) | Plain `*ViewModel` class exposing `StateFlow<*UiState>` (`collectAsStateWithLifecycle`) |
| `@MainActor @Observable final class Store` + `RootView` (+ `RootView+*Destinations.swift`) | `AppCoordinator` (`StateFlow<AppUIState>`) + `RootScreen` (+ `RootScreen*Destinations.kt`) |
| `Route` / `NavigationPath` (`*Navigation`) / `ModalRoute` | Sealed interfaces (`Route`, `*Navigation` inside `List<*Navigation>`, `ModalRoute`) |
| *(Opt-in)* `BottomSheetRoute` + `PendingSheetActions` + `stagedSheetDismissAction` + `RootBottomSheetCoordinator` | *(Opt-in — match iOS)* `BottomSheetRoute` + `PendingSheetActions` + `stagedSheetDismissAction` + `RootBottomSheetCoordinator` |
| `ModelContainer` (disk → in-memory fallback → `StorageUnavailableView`) | `AppDatabase` (`Room.databaseBuilder` → `inMemoryDatabaseBuilder` fallback → `StorageUnavailableScreen`) |
| `ModelContext` injected into ViewModel `init` | Room `@Dao`s + `AppPreferences` injected into ViewModel `constructor` |
| SwiftData `@Model` (`*Entity`) + `save(in:)` upsert + static `load*` + `hasBlobDecodeError` | Room `@Entity` (`*Entity`) + `@Dao` (`@Upsert` + `loadBy*` queries) + resilient JSON blob decoding + `hasBlobDecodeError` |
| Domain `struct *Codable` + dual-write helpers (`RemoteStore` seam) | `@Serializable data class *Codable` + dual-write methods (`toEntity()`, `dao.upsert()`, `saveToFirestore()`, `RemoteStore` seam) — **no Repository layer** |
| `FirestoreRemoteStore` (`RemoteStore.current`: Live in prod, InMemory in tests) | `FirestoreRemoteStore` (`RemoteStore.current`: Live in prod, InMemory in tests) |
| `UserDefaults` + `pendingSyncIds` dirty-sync set + `purgeAppOwnedUserDefaults()` | `AppPreferences` (`SharedPreferences`) + `pendingSyncIds` dirty-sync set + `purgeAppOwnedKeys()` |
| `AuthEmailPasswordValidation` + `PasswordIssue` + `AuthCredentialFirebaseErrorUI` | `AuthEmailPasswordValidation` + `PasswordIssue` + `AuthCredentialFirebaseErrorUI` (verbatim copy & rules — see `ios-auth-validation.mdc`) |
| Firebase iOS SDK (Auth/Firestore/Functions) | Firebase Android SDK (same project, behind `FirestoreRemoteStore` seam) |
| StoreKit 2 | Google Play Billing |
| Local notifications + timers (`UNUserNotificationCenter`) | WorkManager |
| Sign in with Apple / Google | Credential Manager + Google Identity (plus email/anonymous per iOS) |
| Bundled JSON in app bundle (`Models/Schema/`, catalogs) | `app/src/main/assets/*.json` |
| `Testing` framework | JUnit5 (JVM) + AndroidJUnit (instrumented) |

**Backend is shared.** The Android app points at the *same* Firebase project
and the *same* Cloud Functions as iOS. No backend changes are required — only
client parity. Confirm each function’s name, region, and payload during Phase 0.

